    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Cyrus began as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and by 550 BCE had overthrown his overlord and grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astyages">Astyages</a>.</li><li>The conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> and, in 539 BCE, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> gave him the largest empire the world had seen to that point, stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus River</a>.</li><li>Babylonian, Jewish and Greek sources remember him as a tolerant ruler, though historians read the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a> as traditional royal propaganda and not as a charter of human rights.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> calls Cyrus anointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a>, a &quot;messiah&quot;; he is the only non-Jewish figure to be revered in this capacity.</p></aside>
    </section>

