    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 600 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Cyrus is born to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-i">Cambyses I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">King of Anshan</a>, and Mandane, daughter of the Median king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astyages">Astyages</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">559 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He succeeds his father as king, still under Median overlordship</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">550 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After at least three years of hostilities he captures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a> and overthrows the Median kingdom</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">546 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>, occupies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> and conquers the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydian kingdom</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">539 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">His army routs the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opis">Opis</a> and he enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">533 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> and collects tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> cities</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">530 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He dies around December and is succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/astyages"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/astyages.jpg" alt="Astyages" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/astyages">Astyages</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Astyages_submitting_to_Cyrus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Designed by Maximilien de Haese, Woven by Jac. van der Borght (1771-1775), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cyrus was born during the period of 600–599 BCE to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-i">Cambyses I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">King of Anshan</a>, and Mandane, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astyages">Astyages</a>, King of Media. By his own account, now generally believed accurate, he was preceded as king by his father, his grandfather Cyrus I and his great-grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teispes">Teispes</a>, who had taken the title "King of Anshan" after seizing that city. The traditional view holds that he was an Achaemenid, a descendant of Achaemenes, although M. Waters has suggested that his family was of Teispid and Anshanite origin and unrelated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> or to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>. Some scholars hold that his name, Kūruš in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a>, was Elamite in origin.</p><p>The story of his childhood is known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, whose account is mythological. In it Astyages, warned by dreams that his grandson would supplant him, ordered the infant killed; his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harpagus">Harpagus</a> passed the task to a shepherd, who raised the boy as his own. When Cyrus was ten his royal birth was discovered and he was sent back to his parents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, while Astyages punished Harpagus by tricking him into eating his own son. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> says that Cyrus was at first named Agradates.</p><p>Cyrus married Cassandane, an Achaemenian and daughter of Pharnaspes, who bore him two sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Bardiya</a>, and three daughters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atossa">Atossa</a>, Artystone and Roxane. When she died he insisted on public mourning throughout the kingdom; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus-chronicle">Nabonidus Chronicle</a> states that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> mourned her for six days. On his father's death he inherited the Persian throne at Pasargadae, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Astyages.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/croesus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/croesus.jpg" alt="Croesus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Croesus_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marco Prins, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cyrus succeeded to the throne in 559 BCE but, like his predecessors, had to recognise Median overlordship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astyages">Astyages</a> may have ruled most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Ancient Near East</a>, from the Lydian frontier to the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus-chronicle">Nabonidus Chronicle</a>, Astyages attacked Cyrus, "king of Ansan". <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says that the Median commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harpagus">Harpagus</a> encouraged Cyrus to revolt and then defected with several nobles and part of the army, a mutiny the Chronicle confirms. The hostilities lasted at least three years (553–550 BCE) and ended with the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a> and of Astyages. According to Herodotus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a>, Cyrus spared his grandfather's life and married his daughter Amytis. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> now came under his command, and the Chronicle records the change of his title from "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">King of Anshan</a>" to "King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>".</p><p>The dates of the Lydian war are unknown, but it fell between 550 and 539 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, attacked the city of Pteria in Cappadocia and enslaved its inhabitants. Cyrus marched against him, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pteria">Battle of Pteria</a> was effectively a stalemate with heavy casualties on both sides. Croesus withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> and called on his allies, but before they could unite Cyrus besieged him there. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thymbra">Battle of Thymbra</a> Harpagus advised placing dromedaries in front of the army, whose smell frightened the Lydian horses, and the Lydian cavalry was routed. Cyrus occupied Sardis in 546 BCE. Herodotus says he spared Croesus and kept him as an adviser; some translations of the Nabonidus Chronicle say the king of Lydia was slain.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder.jpg" alt="Cyrus Cylinder" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyrus_Cylinder_front.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prioryman, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> fell, a Lydian named Pactyas raised a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>. Cyrus sent Mazares, who subdued Magnesia and Priene, and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harpagus">Harpagus</a>, who took Lycia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolis">Aeolia</a> and Caria and finished the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> in 542 BCE. Between about 545 and 540 BCE Cyrus campaigned in the east, attacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arachosia">Arachosia</a>, Sogdia, Chorasmia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a> and other regions, and in 533 BCE he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> and collected tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> cities.</p><p>By 540 BCE he had captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and its capital, Susa. Just before October 539 BCE his army routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opis">Opis</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a> was seized without a battle on 10 October, the general Gubaru's troops entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> around 12 October and detained King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>, and Cyrus entered the city on 29 October. He proclaimed himself "king of Babylon, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, king of the four corners of the world" on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a>, a clay cylinder deposited in the foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esagila">Esagila</a> temple, which describes how he improved the lives of the Babylonians, repatriated displaced peoples and restored temples.</p><p>His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> formed the largest empire the world had seen to that point, from Asia Minor to the Indus River. He governed it from four capitals, Pasargadae, Babylon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>, allowing regional autonomy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a>. The empire rested on tribute and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>; his army included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immortals">Immortals</a>, a unit of 10,000 highly trained soldiers. He formed a postal system of relay stations, built palaces and irrigated gardens at Pasargadae, and made the Lydian Croeseid the standard gold coin of his empire.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tomyris"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tomyris.jpg" alt="Tomyris" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tomyris">Tomyris</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomyris-Castagno.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrea del Castagno, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cyrus's enemies were the kings he displaced: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astyages">Astyages</a> of Media, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus of Lydia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a> denounces as impious. Not every campaign succeeded: he was decisively defeated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedrosia">Gedrosia</a>, which was most likely conquered only under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>. Pactyas, who led the Lydian revolt, was probably tortured and put to death after his capture. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>'s account the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massagetae">Massagetae</a> queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomyris">Tomyris</a> denounced as underhanded the stratagem by which Cyrus left wine to trap her son's troops.</p><p>Modern debate concerns his reputation for tolerance. The Shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> presented the Cyrus Cylinder in the 1970s as "the first human rights charter in history"; others dispute this as "rather anachronistic", and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> describes the cylinder as "an instrument of ancient Mesopotamian propaganda" in a long tradition of kings opening their reigns with declarations of reforms. Lester Grabbe maintains that his religious policy continued that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, and argues that there was no decree for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>' return but a general policy. Mary Joan Winn Leith writes that appeals to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> show "the Persian tendency to co-opt local religious and political traditions in the interest of imperial control".</p><p>There was also Jewish criticism of Cyrus for halting the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> after the Cuthites accused the Jews of conspiring to rebel; it was not completed until 515 BCE. His own religion is uncertain: Pierre Briant calls it "quite reckless" to reconstruct it, and whether he was a Zoroastrian is debated.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii.jpg" alt="Cambyses II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stela_Cambyses_Apis_closeup.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jona Lendering, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The details of Cyrus's death vary by account. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> has him die in battle with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massagetae">Massagetae</a>, a Scythian confederation, after their queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomyris">Tomyris</a> rejected his offer of marriage and he tried to take her territory by force, about 529 BCE. Cyrus left a camp stocked with wine; Tomyris's son Spargapises and a third of her troops took it, drank and were defeated, and Spargapises killed himself in captivity. Tomyris then led a second force herself, Cyrus was killed in what Herodotus called the fiercest battle of his career, and she had his head dipped in a vessel of blood. Some scholars question this version, since Herodotus admits it was one of many he had heard.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a> says that Cyrus died putting down resistance from the Derbices, north-east of the headwaters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>; Berossus, that he fell fighting the Dahae; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>'s Cyropaedia, contradicting the others, that he died peacefully at his capital. Muhammad Dandamayev says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> may have recovered his body from the Massagetae. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform</a> evidence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> proves that he died around December 530 BCE and that his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> had become king.</p><p>His remains may have been interred at Pasargadae, where a limestone tomb built around 540–530 BCE, which many believe to be his, still stands. Greek accounts describe a golden coffin inside it and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a> stationed to guard it. Cambyses captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> but died after only seven years of rule. He was followed by Cyrus's other son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Bardiya</a>, or an impostor posing as him, who ruled for seven months until he was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xenophon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xenophon.jpg" alt="Xenophon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Xenophon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire Cyrus founded reached its greatest extent under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>, and its system of many countries, religions and languages under one administration was retained by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> and by later Iranian dynasties, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> the peoples he ruled called him "The Father"; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> regarded him as "The Liberator". In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> he is exalted for ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-captivity">Babylonian captivity</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> credits his decree with the return to Zion and the rebuilding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> admired Cyrus from reading Xenophon's Cyropaedia. Finding the tomb at Pasargadae looted, he put its guardians on trial and ordered Aristobulus to restore it. The Cyropaedia was read by thinkers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> owned two copies. Charles Freeman suggests that Cyrus's achievements "ranked far above" those of Alexander, and Richard Nelson Frye writes that he "became the epitome of the great qualities expected of a ruler in antiquity".</p><p>In modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> he remains a cult figure. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-reza-pahlavi">Mohammad Reza Pahlavi</a>, the last Shah, appealed to his legacy in celebrating 2,500 years of monarchy and adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a>, discovered in 1879 and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, as a political symbol. The tomb and Pasargadae are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage site. From the 19th century some Indian Muslim scholars, first Syed Ahmad Khan and then Maulana Azad, identified Cyrus with Dhu al-Qarnayn of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, a theory most Western scholars have not accepted.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Cyrus II rose from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a> to master of Media, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and ruling it through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a>. Ancient accounts disagree on how he died in 530 BCE. Babylonian, Jewish and Greek traditions remember him as a tolerant ruler, a reputation historians treat with caution.</em></p>

